  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nToday and Tonight..\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis this afternoon \ndepict mid- level ridging centered over the Desert Southwest \nwith subsequent troughing in place over the northeastern CONUS. \nNestled between the two, we reside under northwesterly flow \naloft. At the surface, high pressure will slide across the area\ntoday, with calm winds beginning out of the north before \nveering to southerly return flow by this evening. Overall, we \nare in for a pleasant late-August day with temperatures peaking \nin the low to mid 80s. Today looks to be the nicest day of the \nforecast, so take advantage of the pleasant weather and spend \nsome time outdoors if you can! \n\nMid-level cloud cover will increase overnight, though the greater \nchances should hold off until after the partial lunar eclipse. The \nincreasing cloud should limit radiational cooling enough to avoid \nanother morning of fog, though patchy fog may sneak into southwest \nIowa where cloud cover holds off until later in the morning. \n\nFriday...\n\nTwo rounds of thunderstorms will be possible on Friday, with the \nfirst working in during the mid- to late-morning hours as a 30 kt \nLLJ noses into a weak are of surface convergence in eastern \nNebraska. A corridor of 1500-2000 J/kg of MUCAPE will bring the \npotential for some rumbles of thunder and brief, heavy rain showers, \nthough the limited bulk shear should cap overall storm intensity. \nShower coverage will decrease into the afternoon, with pockets of \nsunshine helping to push afternoon high temperatures into the mid to \nupper 80s. \n\nThrough the day, lee troughing will emerge across western SD/NE, \nbringing increasing southerly flow and low-level moisture advection \ninto central and eastern Nebraska. In turn, a modestly unstable, yet \ncapped, airmass will develop from north-central NE and northeast \nNebraska into south-central SD. Convective development and timing \nremain a little more uncertain with this round, given the lack of \nlarge scale forcing for ascent. Recent CAM guidance holds off \ndevelopment until the late evening and early overnight period as the \nLLJ noses into northeast Nebraska. If storms are able to develop, a \nstrong storm or two can't be ruled out, primarily across far northeast\nNebraska, where isolated pockets of damaging wind gusts (up to \n60 mph) and small hail will be possible. A few decaying showers \nand storms may lingering into Saturday morning. \n\nSaturday and Beyond...\n\nWarming temperatures will be the main story this weekend as the \naforementioned mid-level ridge pulls eastward into the central and \nsouthern Plains and lee troughing strengthens over the northern \nPlains. This will push high temperatures into the low 90s on\nSaturday and mid to upper 90s on Sunday. Continued low- level \nmoisture advection will bring dewpoints into the mid to upper \n60s, pushing heat index values to the upper 90s Saturday and\naround 100 on Sunday. In addition, breezy south- southwesterly \nwinds gusts will work in particularly on Saturday where gusts \nmay reach 25-35 mph.\n\nPeriodic chances for showers and storms exist this weekend as weak \nshortwave impulses pinch between the northern periphery of the ridge \nand around the base of the trough/mid-level low sliding east across \nND/Saskatchewan and Manitoba. PoPs currently peak at 15% \nSaturday evening, though the better forcing for ascent will \nreside to our north. \n\nMonday, the mid-level low sliding by to our north will help push a \ncold front through the area. The front will help bring high \ntemperatures into the upper 80s to low 90s, providing some relief \nfrom the weekend heat but still remaining above-average. \nTemperatures will reside in this range through the remainder of the \nwork week, with the return of periodic shower and thunderstorm \nchances.",
